BC-250/gfx1013: flush the compute TLB by rebuilding the runlist.

The compute TLB on this board is never invalidated.
gmc_v10_0_flush_gpu_tlb_pasid() looks for the VMID that owns the PASID in
mmATC_VMID*_PASID_MAPPING, a register that on gfx10 under HWS is never written
-- measured here as 20 of 20 flushes hitting zero VMIDs, and independently
reproduced on separate hardware.

Forcing the invalidation through MMIO does not work: neither the KIQ route nor
the direct register route is acknowledged, and the attempt stalls the
translation unit and resets the GPU.

Measured consequence: hipFree unmaps, hipMalloc reuses the same VA with a new
physical address, the new PTEs are correct in memory, and the GPU keeps
translating through the previous mapping. Confirmed directly -- for 15 of 15
failing pages across 3 runs, the physical address the GPU used is exactly what
that same VA pointed to in an earlier generation.

An invalidation that works does exist on this silicon. A process cannot clear
its own bad translation (60 re-reads, 32 fresh pages, a compute dispatch: all
still 20/20 wrong), but the activation of a second process clears it instantly
(0/20), while merely mapping the same buffer without touching it does not.
That activation is a runlist change -- the hardware scheduler preempts the
queues, rebuilds the runlist and reassigns VMIDs, and must invalidate, or a
process would inherit the previous one's translations along with its VMID.

This asks for that cycle on unmap, so the stale entry never survives to be used.

Result, 36 runs counterbalanced within a single boot, each stamped with its own
execute_queues_cpsch count so the active arm is never in doubt:

  stock     13/18 dirty
  runlist    0/18 dirty      Fisher exact, one-tailed: p = 3.7e-06

Verified active by ftrace, not by a printk: execute_queues_cpsch goes from 6 to
68 calls per run. Zero board errors across all 36 runs, no measurable cost.

Safety: the helper takes dqm_lock, so kfd_flush_tlb() must not call it --
restore_process_queues_nocpsch() and allocate_vmid()/deallocate_vmid() already
hold it, and a deadlock there hangs the machine rather than just the GPU. The
only call site is the unmap ioctl, which holds p->mutex and not dqm_lock (the
same order pqm_create_queue already uses). The helper also returns early if the
device is not a BC-250, if there is no dqm, or if scheduling is not
hardware-managed.

Not finished. Heavier workloads are untested, and preemption under sustained
load is the failure mode an outside report describes on this board (cp queue
preemption time out), which unmap_queues_cpsch escalates straight to
kfd_hws_hang() and a GPU reset. There is also clear room to tune: one cycle per
batch of unmaps instead of one per unmap, or only when a VA range is actually
being reused -- the only case that can produce a stale entry at all.

Applies with: patch -p1 < bc250-flush-tlb-by-runlist.patch  (or git apply)
Generated against a pristine tree; verified to apply on linux 6.19/7.0 and on
torvalds/linux master (offsets only, no fuzz).

